# Mirebridge: a damp, warm town of eel-trappers, reedcutters, peat cutters and herbalists by the Long River in the
# Sallow Fens. The Eel and Lantern and its hundred-year fire, the herbalist, the general store, the chapel on its
# stones and its mist bell, the watch, the causeways, the eel channels, the Drowned Delta further on, and the lights
# that walk the fen at night.

scene rg_fh_peat_fire
when topic=lore here.region=mirebridge
a : The Eel and Lantern's hearth hasn't gone out in a hundred years, they say. | Somebody nearly let the Eel and Lantern's fire die last winter. The whole town turned out. | Every hearth in Mirebridge was lit from the Eel and Lantern once.
b[innkeeper] : My grandmother's grandmother lit it. I'll die before it goes out.
b[old] : The day that fire dies, the fen takes the town. Old saying.
b : A hundred years? | Close thing. | So they say.
a : At least. | Very close. | Every one.

scene rg_fh_marsh_lights
when topic=lore here.region=mirebridge here.time=dusk|night
a : The marsh lights are dancing out past the causeway. | Old Sedge talks to the marsh lights. Swears they answer. | Never follow the lights. Never.
b[old] : The lights are the drowned, looking for the way home.
b[priest] : Marsh gas and fear. Stay on the causeway and pray.
b[child] : They're pretty! Can I go closer?
b : Pretty, from here. | Mad as a heron. | Never.
a : From here. | Quite mad. | Never.

scene rg_fh_running_water
when topic=lore here.region=mirebridge
a : My aunt won't cross running water after dark. Not even the ditch. | They say what walks the fen at night can't cross a running lode. | The old folk build their doors facing the river. For protection.
b[old] : Running water stops what still water hides. Remember that.
b : Old fear. | So they say. | Odd custom.
a : Very old. | So they say. | Very odd.

scene rg_fh_peat_cutting
when topic=work here.region=mirebridge a.job=laborer|miner|farmer
a : Cut three hundred turves today. My back's broken. | Peat's too wet to burn this season. | Found an old wooden spade in the peat. Ancient thing.
b[laborer] : Cut in spring, stack in summer, burn in winter. Never cut wet.
b : Rest it. | Every season. | Older than the town?
a : Can't. | Every one. | Probably.

scene rg_fh_carved_stones
when topic=lore here.region=mirebridge
a : Peat cutters found carved stones under the bog again. | There's a stone in the chapel wall with marks nobody can read. | They say a town lies under the Sallow Fens. Older than any.
b[scholar] : The marks are old script. Older than anything in Ridgemere's Hall of Records, perhaps.
b[priest] : Old stones from the Dark One's age. Best left buried.
b : What did they say? | Nobody at all? | Under the peat?
a : Nobody can read them. | Nobody. | Deep under.

scene rg_fh_bogmyrtle
when topic=smalltalk here.region=mirebridge here.place=street|market|work
a : The herbalist has a cure for everything. Trusts none of them. | Every jar at the herbalist's is labelled in a hand nobody can read. | Bought a root from the herbalist for my fever. Tastes like the fen.
b[healer] : Marsh herbs are strong. Strong cures, strong poisons.
b : That's fen wisdom. | Except the herbalist. | Did it work?
a : It is. | Probably. | I'm still here.

scene rg_fh_apothecary_doubt
when topic=lore here.region=mirebridge pair.band=friends|family|acquaintances
a : They say the herbalist's new tincture made three wolves sick. | Half the cures at the herbalist's could be poison. | I wouldn't take anything from the herbalist's top shelf.
b[healer] : Poison and cure are the same root at different doses.
b : I'd heard. | Probably. | Nor would I.
a : Careful, then. | Very careful. | Nor anyone.

scene rg_fh_stilt_chapel
when topic=lore here.region=mirebridge
a : The chapel bell rang for the mist all night. | The flood came up to the chapel steps. Not past. | They built the chapel on stones after the old one drowned.
b[priest] : The bell guides anyone caught on the causeways in the mist.
b[old] : The old chapel's under the fen still. You can hear its bell some nights.
b : Long night. | Never past. | Wise of them.
a : Very long. | Never yet. | Very wise.

scene rg_fh_eel_traps
when topic=work here.region=mirebridge a.job=fisher|laborer|farmer
a : Eel traps full in the north lode this morning. | Somebody's been emptying my traps before dawn. | Eels are fat this autumn.
b[fisher] : Wicker traps, willow bait, and patience.
b : Good haul. | Thieves. | Good eating.
a : Very good. | Thieves. | Very good.

scene rg_fh_lost_child
when topic=lore here.region=mirebridge pair.band=friends|family|spouses|acquaintances
a : Widow Mire still walks the causeway every dusk. Looking. | The mire took a child three winters ago. She never stopped looking. | Keep your pups off the soft ground.
b[priest] : Grief walks paths that never end. Be kind to her.
b : Poor soul. | How terrible. | I always do.
a : Rain or shine. | Three winters, and still. | Good.

scene rg_fh_rushlights
when topic=smalltalk here.region=mirebridge here.time=dusk|night
a : The general store sold out of rushlights before the long nights. | Rushlights smoke worse than peat. | Fen nights feel longer than anywhere.
b[merchant] : Rushlights are cheap light. Tallow's for those with coin.
b : Every winter. | Cheap light. | Feels that way.
a : Every winter. | Very cheap. | It does.

scene rg_fh_floods
when topic=lore|weather here.region=mirebridge here.weather=rain|storm|overcast
a : The Long River's over the low causeway again. | The lode's rising. Move the peat stacks. | Half the town's floors are wet.
b[old] : The fen gives back what we take. Every winter.
b[guard] : Nobody crosses the low causeway till it drops.
b : Again? | Already did. | Every year.
a : Again. | Good. | Every year.

scene rg_fh_peat_smell
when topic=smalltalk here.region=mirebridge
a : You can smell a Mirebridge wolf a mile off. Peat smoke. | My coat smells of peat even after a week in Ridgemere's rain. | Peat smoke gets into everything.
b[old] : The smell is home. Wear it proud.
b : Proud of it. | It never goes. | Even the bread.
a : Very proud. | Never. | Even the bread.

scene rg_fh_reading
when topic=lore here.region=mirebridge
a : Hardly anyone in Mirebridge can read. | The herbalist's the only one who reads the old labels. | Somebody left a book at the Eel and Lantern. Nobody could tell what it was.
b[scholar] : I could teach. Nobody wants to learn.
b[priest] : The chapel teaches letters on Restday. Nobody comes.
b : Never needed to. | Lucky for them. | Strange.
a : Never. | Lucky. | Very strange.

scene rg_fh_herb_gatherers
when topic=work here.region=mirebridge a.job=healer|farmer|laborer
a : Gathered bogbean and marsh mallow all morning. | The best herbs grow in the worst places. | Saw something in the sedge watching me while I gathered.
b[healer] : Pick at dawn, dry in shade. The marsh herbs keep their strength.
b : Good haul. | Always. | A heron.
a : Very good. | Always. | Probably.

scene rg_fh_marsh_watch
when topic=work here.region=mirebridge a.role=guard
a : Nothing happens in Mirebridge. That's the trouble. | The watch here has two spears and a goose. | Walked the causeway patrol in the mist. Saw nothing. Heard everything.
b[guard] : Quiet's good. Quiet's the job.
b : Lucky you. | And a good goose. | That's the fen.
a : Lucky. | A very good goose. | It is.

scene rg_fh_mud
when topic=smalltalk here.region=mirebridge
a : Mud on every floor in Mirebridge. You stop noticing. | Lost a boot to the mire. | Even the chapel floor's muddy.
b[child] : I like mud! It's squishy!
b : That's the fen. | Gone for good. | Especially there.
a : It is. | Gone. | Especially.

scene rg_fh_mist_bell
when topic=lore|weather here.region=mirebridge here.weather=fog
a : Fog so thick you can't see the causeway stones. | Stay inside till the mist bell stops. | They're ringing the mist bell. Someone's out there.
b[old] : Follow the bell, never the lights.
b : Stay home. | I will. | Poor soul.
a : I will. | I will. | Poor soul.

scene rg_fh_cure_poison
when topic=lore here.region=mirebridge
a : They say the fen cure for fever is the same root that kills cattle. | Somebody sells a love potion at the Eel and Lantern. Smells like bog water. | Every fen cure is half a poison.
b[healer] : Dose is everything. The fen teaches that early.
b : Strange cure. | Probably is bog water. | And half a prayer.
a : Very strange. | Probably. | Half a prayer.

scene rg_fh_frogs
when topic=smalltalk here.region=mirebridge here.season=spring|summer
a : The frogs sang so loud last night I couldn't sleep. | Spawn in every ditch. Spring's here. | Frog legs at the Eel and Lantern tonight.
b[cook] : Frog legs in butter. Don't make a face. They're good.
b : Every spring. | Finally. | No thanks.
a : Every spring. | Finally. | Your loss.

scene rg_fh_stilts
when topic=lore|smalltalk here.region=mirebridge
a : Every house on the low side's on stilts now. | They raised my aunt's house a full stride last summer. | The old houses sank. You can see their roofs in the bog.
b[crafter] : Oak stilts last. Pine rots in the wet.
b : Wise. | Good thing. | Creepy.
a : Very. | Very good. | Very creepy.

scene rg_fh_bog_body
when topic=lore here.region=mirebridge
a : Peat cutters found a body in the bog. Old. Very old. | They say the bog keeps the dead perfect for a thousand years. | Found a leather shoe in the peat. Small. A pup's.
b[priest] : We buried it in the chapel yard. Whoever it was.
b[old] : The bog keeps everything. Even secrets.
b : How old? | So they say. | Sad.
a : Very old. | Perfect. | Very sad.

scene rg_fh_mirelands
when topic=lore here.region=mirebridge
a : Nobody goes past the last cairn into the Drowned Delta. | The Drowned Delta stretches further than anyone's walked. | Somebody went down into the Delta for herbs. Came back three days later, raving.
b[old] : The cairns mark the safe paths. Past the last, there are none.
b : Wise. | Endless. | Poor soul.
a : Very wise. | Endless. | Poor soul.

scene rg_fh_dragonflies
when topic=smalltalk here.region=mirebridge here.season=summer
a : Dragonflies thick as smoke over the lode. | The midges are eating me alive. | Hot and damp. Fen summer.
b[child] : I caught a blue one! It flew away.
b : Pretty, though. | Burn some peat. | The worst.
a : Very pretty. | I am. | The worst.

scene rg_fh_sedge_basket
when topic=work|smalltalk here.region=mirebridge
a : Wove six sedge baskets for the store. | Sedge mats sell well in the towns. | Peat spades are dearer than ever.
b[crafter] : Sedge is free. The weaving's what costs.
b : Good work. | Cheap trade. | Everything is.
a : Thank you. | Honest trade. | Everything.

scene rg_fh_tavern_tales
when topic=smalltalk here.region=mirebridge here.place=tavern
a : Somebody's telling the bog-wolf story again at the Eel and Lantern. | Peat smoke so thick in the Eel and Lantern you can't see the bar. | The Eel and Lantern's floor is half mud, half straw.
b[innkeeper] : That fire's older than any of your stories.
b : Not again. | Like a fog. | Better than bare mud.
a : Every night. | Thick as fog. | Barely.

scene rg_fh_causeway
when topic=lore|work here.region=mirebridge
a : They're laying new stones on the north causeway. | The causeway sank a hand's breadth since spring. | Only the causeways keep the fen from swallowing us.
b[laborer] : Lay a stone, the fen eats a stone. Lay another.
b : About time. | It always does. | They do.
a : About time. | Always. | They do.

scene rg_fh_willow_wands
when topic=lore here.region=mirebridge
a : My grandmother tied a willow wand over every door. | They hang willow over cradles in Mirebridge. For the mire. | Fen folk carry a willow wand at night.
b[old] : Willow bends and never breaks. Like the fen folk.
b : Old custom. | Lovely custom. | I do.
a : Very old. | Very lovely. | Good.

scene rg_fh_bittern
when topic=lore here.region=mirebridge here.time=dusk|night
a : The bittern's booming in the reeds. | Old folk say the bittern's boom is the bog's heart. | Heard the bittern three nights running.
b[old] : When the bittern stops, the fen's dying.
b : Eerie sound. | Strange saying. | Good sign.
a : Very eerie. | Very strange. | I hope.

scene mb_long_river
when topic=lore|smalltalk here.region=mirebridge
a : They say the Long River starts in a cold lake under the mountains. Hard to believe in this heat. | The river's brown and fat today. Rain upstream. | Fished a carved float out of the river. Came all the way from Coldwater, I'd bet.
b[old] : Cold up there, warm down here. Same water.
b[fisher] : Whatever the river carries, the eels find first.
b : Hard to believe. | Mind the bridge. | Long way to float.
a : It is. | I will. | A very long way.

scene mb_reedcutters
when topic=work here.region=mirebridge a.job=laborer|farmer|crafter
a : Cut reed by the river till my paws went soft. | Reed's tall as a wolf this year. | Every roof in the south wants Mirebridge reed.
b[laborer] : Cut in the cold months, when the leaves are off. Summer reed rots.
b : Rest them. | Good year. | Good trade.
a : Can't. | Very good. | Steady, anyway.

scene mb_clay_pits
when topic=work|smalltalk here.region=mirebridge
a : Dug clay at the pits all morning. I'm grey to the elbow. | The clay pits flooded again. | Fen clay makes the best pots in the south, they say.
b[crafter] : Fen clay is fine and sticky. Fire it slow or it cracks.
b : Wash it off. | They always do. | So they say.
a : Later. | Always. | So they say.
